/* book exam traps: 0 mA is a fault, not a zero reading. Live zero means bottom of range is 4 mA. | The current is the same everywhere in the loop; wire resistance changes voltage, not current. | A 2-wire RTD reads high by its lead resistance; only a 4-wire connection removes it fully. | Rloop,max counts cable both ways plus the sense resistor, not cable one way. | 4 mA is a real offset (the live zero). It is removed at the receiver when voltage is scaled to engineering units, not by changing anything in the loop. */
