/* book Worked example 2: Q: The cylinder of the first example (50 mm bore, 6 bar, efficiency 0.9) has a 20 mm rod. (a) Find the retraction force. (b) Find the theoretical force of a 50 mm hydraulic cylinder at 150 bar and compare it with the pneumatic one.
   1. (a) On the rod side the air acts on the annulus: A = π(D² − d²)/4 = π(0.050² − 0.020²)/4 = π × 0.0021 / 4 = 1.649 × 10⁻³ m².
   2. Retraction force: F = η p A = 0.9 × 6 × 10⁵ × 1.649 × 10⁻³ = 890.6 N, against 1060 N when extending.
   3. (b) Hydraulic: F = p A = 150 × 10⁵ Pa × 1.963 × 10⁻³ m² = 29 450 N = 29.4 kN.
   4. Ratio of theoretical forces: 29 450 / 1178 = 25.0, which is simply the pressure ratio 150 / 6.
   5. Check: the rod takes (0.020/0.050)² = 16 % of the piston area, and 890.6 / 1060 = 0.84, so the retraction force is 16 % lower, as expected.
   Answer: Retraction gives 890.6 N; the hydraulic cylinder gives 29.4 kN, 25 times the pneumatic theoretical force. */
